<Base title="About" tab="about">
  <h1>About these compilations</h1>
  <div class="rules">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>A <strong>compiled transcription</strong> is one text of a book in the language it was written in, built from the transcriptions of its surviving manuscripts, aiming at the words as the author wrote them.</p>
    <h2>How it is made</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>There is one <a href="/specification/">specification</a>, <code>SPECIFICATION.md</code>. It states the goal and what an output must contain, and nothing about how.</li>
      <li>The compiler chooses its own method, including a statistical model for how likely each reading is to be original, and writes that method into its output with the evidence for every reading.</li>
      <li>Each compilation is one output, filed by the specification's hash and the compiler's name. The compiler identifies itself in it. The same inputs always give the same output.</li>
      <li>A quick check, which takes about a second, confirms that there is one specification, that each output matches its recorded hashes, that every printed word is in a manuscript, that no word is doubled unless a manuscript doubles it, and that every chance lies inside its range.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>What is used, and what is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Used:</strong> transcriptions of ancient manuscripts in the original languages, and the facts their holders record about them (such as a date range), listed in <code>transcriptions/manuscripts.json</code>. The books and the folders each section reads are listed in <code>transcriptions/books.json</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Not used:</strong> printed or critical editions, modern reconstructions, translations into other languages, quotations by later writers, or images. A line no manuscript has is shown as missing, never filled in.</li>
      <li><strong>Images</strong> stay with the libraries that hold them. Each manuscript links to its holder's viewer where one exists, and to the holder's own transcription file.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>What each section reads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Old Testament (Hebrew and Aramaic):</strong> the Leningrad Codex, two Samaritan Pentateuch manuscripts, and the Dead Sea Scrolls. The parts in Aramaic are compiled the same way and marked. Hebrew is shown right to left; a switch on each chapter shows the consonants only.</li>
      <li><strong>New Testament (Greek):</strong> Greek continuous-text manuscripts, Greek lectionaries and the biblical text of Greek catena manuscripts.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Words used on this site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>manuscript</strong> is a surviving handwritten copy, whole or in pieces.</li>
      <li>A <strong>transcription</strong> is the letters of one manuscript, typed.</li>
      <li>A <strong>line</strong> is the numbered unit used for reference, written chapter:line.</li>
      <li>A <strong>reading</strong> is what one manuscript has at one point; a <strong>place</strong> is a stretch where the manuscripts do not all agree.</li>
      <li>A <strong>chance</strong> is how likely a reading is to be what the author wrote, by the output's own method; its <strong>90% range</strong> shows how sure that estimate is.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
